<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Jerusalem (1187)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1187">1187</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as reconquest of Jerusalem, reconquer Jerusalem</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of Jerusalem in 1187 was the recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> after his victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a>. The city surrendered on terms that allowed many inhabitants to ransom themselves. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> treat the reconquest as the culmination of Saladin's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> and the source of his lasting prestige.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, weakened by internal disputes, was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> on 4 July 1187. Most of its nobility were taken prisoner, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">King Guy</a> among them. By mid-September <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a>, Toron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> held out thanks to the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a>. Balian of Ibelin asked Saladin for safe passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to fetch his wife, Maria Komnene, and their family. He was allowed to go on condition that he not bear arms or stay more than a day, but the patriarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> and the inhabitants begged him to organise the defence, and Heraclius absolved him of his oath.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The city was crowded with refugees and had fewer than fourteen knights, so Balian created sixty more from squires and burgesses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> arrived on 20 September. For six days his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">siege towers</a> failed to take the walls. On 26 September he moved his camp to the Mount of Olives, where there was no major gate from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> could sally, and a mined section of the wall collapsed on 29 September. Neither side could gain the breach. The clergy organised a barefoot procession round the walls, and the civilians were in despair.</p><p>At the end of September Balian rode out to offer surrender. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> insisted on an unconditional surrender and said he had sworn to take the city by force, until Balian threatened that the defenders would destroy the Muslim holy places and kill their own families and the 5,000 Muslim slaves. Saladin then agreed to a ransom of ten dinars for men, five for women and two for children, and accepted 30,000 dinars for 7,000 of the poor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Balian surrendered the city on 2 October 1187. The takeover was relatively peaceful, in contrast to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> capture of 1099. About 15,000 who could not pay were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adil">al-Adil</a> freed many. The Christian cross on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> was pulled down. Saladin spared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>, allowed local Christians to stay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to resettle, and charged Frankish pilgrims a fee. Control of Christian affairs passed to the patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> continued, with its capital moved first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>. On 29 October Pope Gregory VIII issued the bull Audita tremendi, and in 1189 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> and Frederick I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Jerusalem (1187)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ayyubid sultans patronised religious scholarship, endowed madrasas that consolidated Sunni learning in former Fatimid Egypt, and restored the Dome of the Rock and the al-Aqsa Mosque to Muslim worship after the <b>reconquest of Jerusalem</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
